  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 139 PM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nWeak zonal flow aloft with high pressure will continue to dominate \nthe weather pattern through Sunday. This continues to bring benign \nconditions today and Sunday, with calmer winds and cooler \ntemperatures. With recent the snowfall and some melting snow \ntoday, there will be lingering low level moisture across the \nregion. Another morning of calm winds and high pressure can \ndevelop fog and/or low stratus clouds across the region Sunday \nmorning.\n\nMonday, a closed upper-level low moves onshore of the Pacific \nNorthwest, with the wave opening back up as it propagates east. This \nnext incoming system brings snow to the higher elevations and \nrain/snow to the lower elevations. There remains some uncertainty \nin how much cold air aloft this system brings, but low \ntemperatures Tuesday morning below freezing can allow for light \nsnow to mix down to lower elevations. \n\nEnsembles hint at an unsettled, cooler pattern sticking around \nthrough next weekend as a continuous line of shortwave trough \nmove across the Northwest U.S. Generally the pattern shows \nmountain snow with rain at lower elevations during the day, and \nlight snow at night.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFog Sunday morning:\n\nBoth forecast soundings and probabilistic guidance supports the \npotential for fog and stratus developing for isolated portions of \nthe region, with highest chances in Cascade and Lewis & Clark \nCounties. Here, there's a 20-60% chance for fog development, with a \n20-30% chance for fog dropping visibility to below a mile. One \ncaveat is there is also a 20-60% chance for low stratus to \ndevelop, which may limit overall fog development.\n\nStorm System Early Next Week:\n\nEnsembles don't hint at this system being a big snow producer for \neither the mountains or lower elevations. This system tries to pull \nin colder air aloft, but it's not very rigorous. Snow levels try \nto fall to the lower elevations overnight Monday into Tuesday \nmorning, which will allow for some snow to reach the lower \nelevations. However, current trends have the wet bulb \ntemperatures rise above freezing, limiting overall impacts at \nlower elevations. -Wilson",
